Coastal wetlands, including salt marshes, mangroves, and swamps, act as nature’s first line of defense against storm surges and coastal flooding. Their ability to mitigate damage is not merely a matter of physical barrier but involves complex hydrodynamic interactions that dissipate energy before it reaches human infrastructure.

The primary physical mechanism at work is friction. As storm-driven waves and rising water levels move inland, they must push through the dense vegetation of the wetland. Stems, roots, and leaves create significant drag on the water, slowing its velocity. This deceleration is crucial because the energy of moving water is